00:03:18.826 --> 00:03:20.406
engage in drug abuse.

00:03:20.407 --> 00:03:24.509
So this is a network map and
what you see here is 160

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:29.655
homeless youth who are connected
to each other in a social space.

00:03:29.656 --> 00:03:33.981
And the blue dots are people,
all of the dots are people and

00:03:33.982 --> 00:03:38.147
the lines are basically
the relationships among them.

00:03:38.148 --> 00:03:40.355
And the blue nodes
are people who engage,

00:03:40.356 --> 00:03:42.390
youth who engage in drug use.

00:03:42.391 --> 00:03:43.460
And the grey and

00:03:43.461 --> 00:03:46.218
white dots are people who
don't engage in drug use.

00:03:46.219 --> 00:03:48.029
And what you'll
see here is that,

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:50.971
really drug use is a public
health phenomenon, right?

00:03:50.972 --> 00:03:54.686
All the drug users tend
to cluster together,

00:03:54.687 --> 00:03:58.990
which is explained by
classic social theories.

00:03:58.991 --> 00:04:00.330
Homophily, the notion that

00:04:00.331 --> 00:04:02.500
birds of the same
feather flock together.

00:04:02.501 --> 00:04:03.850
And also social learning theory,

00:04:03.851 --> 00:04:06.100
if you're around people
who engage in a behavior,

00:04:06.101 --> 00:04:08.390
guess what, you're more likely
to engage in that behavior.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:14.620
So, however, the ironic thing is
that in terms of preventing drug

00:04:14.621 --> 00:04:18.451
use, even though networks
are implicated in how,

00:04:18.452 --> 00:04:20.780
why substance abuse happens.

00:04:20.781 --> 00:04:25.520
Networks are also the key
to preventing drug abuse.

00:04:25.521 --> 00:04:29.870
So what research has found that
prevention programs are most

00:04:29.871 --> 00:04:33.025
effective when they
have a peer component,

00:04:33.026 --> 00:04:37.203
because then people get to
engage in drug refusal skills or

00:04:37.204 --> 00:04:41.573
drug resistance skills in their
natural peer environment.

00:04:41.574 --> 00:04:45.613
So the way these peer network
interventions have been designed

00:04:45.614 --> 00:04:49.041
is that people are usually
assigned to groups based on

00:04:49.042 --> 00:04:52.394
random assignment without
any knowledge of how they

00:04:52.395 --> 00:04:54.732
are connecting to their peers.

00:04:54.733 --> 00:04:58.730
Or also, not an understanding
of the social space

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:00.810
where they're
engaging in drug use.

00:05:00.811 --> 00:05:04.445
Or the other method that is used
is that youth get to choose

00:05:04.446 --> 00:05:05.972
their own peer groups.

00:05:05.973 --> 00:05:08.217
And if you go back to
the previous slide,

00:05:08.218 --> 00:05:11.319
what we saw is that peers tend
to hang out with other peers

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:12.788
who are like themselves.

00:05:12.789 --> 00:05:17.050
So a lot of times what happens
is that a lot of users come to

00:05:17.051 --> 00:05:21.953
get together and that increases
their drug use behavior which is

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:25.461
known by the term called
Deviancy Training.

00:05:25.462 --> 00:05:28.550
And Deviancy Training is
a problem in prevention science.

00:05:28.551 --> 00:05:31.930
It has been a sticky problem in
prevention science ever since we

00:05:31.931 --> 00:05:36.920
have been trying to prevent drug
abuse among any groups of youth.

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:42.150
So in terms of the question we
wanted to address or the problem

00:05:42.151 --> 00:05:46.000
we wanted to address through our
interdisciplinary collaboration

00:05:46.001 --> 00:05:51.580
is, how to assign people to
groups based on their existing

00:05:51.581 --> 00:05:55.800
network relationships, and also
their substance use behaviors.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:03.050
And the way we saw how AI
helped is that with AI,

00:06:03.051 --> 00:06:06.850
we wanted to understand whether
we could have a predictive

00:06:06.851 --> 00:06:10.610
algorithm that could tell us
whether or not if we engage in

00:06:10.611 --> 00:06:13.990
that intervention, if Deviancy
Training would be exacerbated.

00:06:13.991 --> 00:06:16.450
Or if you could really
use Deviancy Training.

00:06:16.451 --> 00:06:17.963
So in the context of AI,

00:06:17.964 --> 00:06:21.310
we really saw this as
a graph partitioning problem.

00:06:21.311 --> 00:06:25.756
So if you go back to the figure
that I showed you earlier,

00:06:25.757 --> 00:06:28.594
what if we could
design an algorithm

00:06:28.595 --> 00:06:31.527
that could reconfigure
this network

00:06:31.528 --> 00:06:36.068
based on drug use as well as
preexisting relationships,

00:06:36.069 --> 00:06:40.348
in a way where we could
optimize positive influence.

00:06:40.349 --> 00:06:44.638
So instead of cutting these
networks into clusters,

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:49.404
we had to reconfigure people in
a way that we could basically

00:06:49.405 --> 00:06:52.854
reduce the problem of
deviancy training.

00:06:54.784 --> 00:06:58.256
So in order to do that,
we thought we had to model three

00:06:58.257 --> 00:07:02.583
important parameters, and I'm
gonna talk about what parameters

00:07:02.584 --> 00:07:06.215
we included and not really
the simulation techniques,

00:07:06.216 --> 00:07:09.170
and Phoebe's gonna
address that in her doc.

00:07:09.171 --> 00:07:12.573
But in terms of understanding
why Deviancy Training happens

00:07:12.574 --> 00:07:13.906
and how to mitigate it,

00:07:13.907 --> 00:07:17.177
we thought there are three
important modeling components

00:07:17.178 --> 00:07:20.040
that we needed to include
in our algorithm.

00:07:20.041 --> 00:07:23.000
First, is Network Influence
which I'm gonna talk about.

00:07:23.001 --> 00:07:25.260
The second,
is Network Dynamic Modeling and

00:07:25.261 --> 00:07:27.080
third is
Interventionist Modeling.

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:32.920
So in terms of Influence
Modeling, as Amalia was saying

00:07:32.921 --> 00:07:36.630
earlier, there are competing
influences in that perks there.

00:07:36.631 --> 00:07:39.139
People are associated
with a lot of people and

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:42.376
because of the diversity of
these influences they are both

00:07:42.377 --> 00:07:44.964
positive and
negative influences on people.

00:07:44.965 --> 00:07:48.626
So the way we saw it is that,
it is linear threshold model.

00:07:48.627 --> 00:07:51.506
So basically people
get both positive and

00:07:51.507 --> 00:07:54.549
negative influences
from their peers, and

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:58.334
if the influence signal
crosses the threshold value,

00:07:58.335 --> 00:08:02.540
then the person is more likely
to change their behavior.

00:08:02.541 --> 00:08:07.150
So for example, if a user is
connected to more non-users

00:08:07.151 --> 00:08:09.900
than users, they're more likely
to change their behavior.

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:16.110
Also, because this intervention
the way we design social work

00:08:16.111 --> 00:08:19.800
intervention is that it happens
over a period of time, and

00:08:19.801 --> 00:08:23.090
over that period of time people
change their behaviors and

00:08:23.091 --> 00:08:24.380
also their relationships.

00:08:24.381 --> 00:08:28.290
It was important for us to model
how those relationships change.

00:08:28.291 --> 00:08:33.150
And what we hypothesized is that
if a user became a non-user,

00:08:33.151 --> 00:08:35.860
they're probably more
likely to strengthen their

00:08:35.861 --> 00:08:38.320
relationships with
other non-users and

00:08:38.321 --> 00:08:41.140
weaken their relationships
with other users.

00:08:41.141 --> 00:08:41.980
And that's something we

00:08:41.981 --> 00:08:44.040
also wanted to model
in the algorithm.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:48.270
Also there is the
interventionist components in

00:08:48.271 --> 00:08:49.290
social work.

00:08:49.291 --> 00:08:53.090
Usually, interventions are
delivered by professionals, and

00:08:53.091 --> 00:08:57.020
they are also a key component
of the intervention.

00:08:57.021 --> 00:09:00.450
And we wanted to basically
understand what kind of

00:09:00.451 --> 00:09:03.460
relationships the participants
have with the interventionist,

00:09:03.461 --> 00:09:06.250
and how that changes the nature
of their behaviors as well as

00:09:06.251 --> 00:09:07.010
their networks.

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:12.548
In terms of the results, what we
found is that GUIDE outperforms

00:09:12.549 --> 00:09:16.121
the traditional status quo
methods of random assignment,

00:09:16.122 --> 00:09:18.630
and peer chosen
groups by almost 40%.

00:09:18.631 --> 00:09:21.645
That's pretty huge, and
that's statistically and

00:09:21.646 --> 00:09:23.186
practically meaningful.

00:09:23.187 --> 00:09:27.617
And what this emphasis is that
it's important to acknowledge

00:09:27.618 --> 00:09:31.452
existing relationships and
network tie strength and

00:09:31.453 --> 00:09:36.324
behavior in understanding how we
can minimize Deviancy Training.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:38.669
How GUIDE helps?

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:43.053
In the real world, we are
planning to deploy this decision

00:09:43.054 --> 00:09:47.705
aid in a shelter in Denver,
Colorado, and what we'll be able

00:09:47.706 --> 00:09:51.910
to do is to predict scenarios
where Deviancy Training is

00:09:51.911 --> 00:09:55.950
increased thus, saving time,
money, and effort.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:00.390
Future strategies, real world
deployment that I talked about.

00:10:00.391 --> 00:10:04.800
We also need to make certain
tweaks to our model to

00:10:04.801 --> 00:10:06.700
kind of include
uncertainties and

00:10:06.701 --> 00:10:09.890
we also need to make our
model a little more complex,

00:10:09.891 --> 00:10:12.570
because right now, we are just
modelling three things.

00:10:12.571 --> 00:10:16.295
We hope to model other
behavioral parameters, and

00:10:16.296 --> 00:10:21.068
demographic variables that might
also impact substance abuse.

00:10:21.069 --> 00:10:23.465
So that concludes
my presentation.

00:10:23.466 --> 00:10:25.830
I'll be happy to talk
to you later and

00:10:25.831 --> 00:10:28.820
answer any questions
you have right now.

00:10:28.821 --> 00:10:32.068
Thank you.
